    "seconds": 22,
    "summary": "The anomaly is mitigated: the `profile` deployment was running the wrong binary, and it now runs `profile`.\n\n**What I found**\n- All pods in `test-hotel-reservation` were `Running`, but the `profile` deployment had just been rolled out with `command: [\"geo\"]` and `containerPort: 8083`.\n- Its logs showed it starting the geo server and registering as `srv-geo` in Consul. Nothing was listening on 8081, so the `profile` Service had an endpoint (`10.244.1.120:8081`) that couldn't serve profile requests.\n- The other services all run their own binary from the same image, so `geo` in the profile spec was the outlier.\n\n**What I changed**\n- Patched `deployment/profile` to set `command` to `[\"profile\"]` and the container port to `8081`. Nothing else changed.\n- The rollout finished successfully. The new pod `profile-8b6dd7c6c-h4z2f` logs `Starting server...` and `Successfully registered in consul` as `srv-profile` at `10.244.1.122:8081`.\n- The `profile` Service now has endpoint `10.244.1.122:8081`, and no pods in the namespace are unhealthy.\n\nI didn't send a test request through the frontend or the profile API, so the fix is verified by logs and endpoints, not end-to-end traffic.",
